# Biomaterials and Tissue
#
# Material classes compared by how they degrade, how the host answers them, and what they are used for, then how cells feel stiffness and why thick tissue needs vessels; bulk materials science lives in physical materials.

#>bioengineering_biomaterials
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!bioengineering
#:form #!comparison
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Biomaterials and Tissue"
 ~#claim: "A biomaterial is chosen by how it degrades and how the host responds, from the first protein layer to the fibrous capsule, and cells read its stiffness through adhesions that pull."
 ~#proof: "Anderson and Shive on PLGA, Tamada and Langer on erosion, Vroman on protein exchange, Anderson on the foreign body reaction, Engler and Discher on stiffness, Dupont on YAP and TAZ."
 ~#door: "Read the matrix row by row, then the host response sequence, then the stiffness contest, then the vessel problem."
 ~#audience: "advanced engineering and biology students"
 continuity: "Biomaterials and Tissue | how the host responds"
 cold_open: "A biomaterial is chosen less for its strength than for how it breaks down and how the host responds to it."
}

